2186045417] [17592186514343 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186513820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514343 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eff8af59-c1b3-5a16-b640-492f487e00fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514346 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514346 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514346 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186513806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514346 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514346 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514346 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514346 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514346 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186514186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514346 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4d40d9e-5b20-5652-9681-0c46dcea53cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514364 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cb/cb757f7a8b81147203ca1e58f040f4327f5f4c92971b9e0f1cf11f1c4fadcdfb.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09d173fe-eece-5b8b-9031-7816fb223b07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514364 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vectors $b$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb62100b65cf97e42cce7e1ce4dc0923fa291f4684b720369b1cdbd21a4393fd.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ and $c$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514376 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6b/6bc83d9194c0d6728e875d4089c97eb92ea45d3d2ef735f882dafe3991b9d2ec.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e02af7d-5317-58cc-b4e8-ba89b18e708e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514376 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vectors $a$ or $c,$ as shown below. So, either $a$ or $c$ define the direction of the first principal component.  \n\n![](images/dd/dd4ca330f3e37334133a6cde00220b872a34afe3bf9d8c5c76f37111c30b8291.png)\n\n  \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $b,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab3d8561432f3862503eb4b213347d11dc2158deeade50a6418867b6f2b03dc3.png)\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514378 #edb/ref 1071 "A neuroscientist measured two specific features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each brain image of a group of patients he is studying. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular brain image. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 \\\\ -0.707\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the neuroscientist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2381e2c6-90e5-5e9a-8022-1f6b70cb4895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514378 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/70/70a37afaaad54e3f384ad446ab2cf57bc0f21ffa8f6986f01841d9f4041f531c.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ -0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ -0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2} & -0.707{x}_{1}+0.707{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514393 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514393 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514393 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514393 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6b/6bc83d9194c0d6728e875d4089c97eb92ea45d3d2ef735f882dafe3991b9d2ec.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514393 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3eab3230-c0ce-5edb-86e7-28c4e5295d2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514393 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vectors $a$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98ccbf23301a3c150409103a4f4a80534acadc365d4335e7f70435cd4d70fde0.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$a$ and $c$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514397 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514397 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514397 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514397 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/79/79bae249d69216842f29df29c6b661d2a8e3aa684300bc6e15433d8a16fb5006.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514397 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47ab5bde-d639-547f-ad10-9ccc080ffe38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514397 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $a,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/15/15d7060e172c87e42a6c69aa90e8f1cdfa649bdf21478f498b7b159f82aa8a68.png)\n\n \nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$a$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514401 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514401 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514401 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514401 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9f/9f4f2f9c00a83968fb22d5fc22523db760bec0f903c692150683fbdb853f5027.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514401 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "525ecf36-0f83-5c22-a5cd-0246fa04eb42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514401 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $c,$ as shown below. So, $c$ defines the direction of the first principal component.\n\n![](images/de/deb970bb184d902257c8610f7de8135abc2ba0461c614fa6508dbe1995964a2f.png)\n\n   \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $b,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/1a/1aaf90a34611401b2b4786b29077f192836aad060b6aca69764f165e20857217.png)\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344087" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514415 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514415 #edb/ref 1071 "A physicist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each electron released in an atomic experiment. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular electron. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.555 \\\\ 0.832\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.832 \\\\ 0.555\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the physicist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68cd1b00-9920-5784-9707-6f682f05d636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514415 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/17/171a9c86bf510c2ee5b65325d6f8939c65b5118286057934f91a053f75faf82f.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.555 & 0.832 \\\\ 0.832 & 0.555\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.555 & 0.832 \\\\ 0.832 & 0.555\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2} & 0.832{x}_{1}+0.555{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514430 #edb/ref 1071 "A physicist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each oscillation produced when a mass hanging on a spring is displaced and then released. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular oscillation. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.456 \\\\ 0.890\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.890 \\\\ -0.456\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the physicist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7eb658cd-5383-5f28-8b76-9d708b2f6422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514430 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/f0/f0f0623703d1a3767a7c3efd275e977c6933465faeb761991f00da705bac4a2c.png)\n\n \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.456 & 0.890 \\\\ 0.890 & -0.456\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.456 & 0.890 \\\\ 0.890 & -0.456\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2} & 0.890{x}_{1}-0.456{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514432 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514432 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514432 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514432 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/13/137e230421e20e7694526973fd4a92f9d573f1d97273151504df940b955d48f9.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the first principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81392296-c859-50aa-9dad-b2d6c26af157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514432 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vectors $a$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5efefe99cb4fe0680d8ea3aaa31a05767f187e54acfb5b28145194e7dec24f3b.png)\n\n \nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$a$ and $c$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514445 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514445 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a8/a84a2fa07d06dce3026740be2831127fd4d7f2d72fd31388df646ad7ac62e4ff.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a40aacc4-bbfa-50e9-8365-3467adc8fc1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514445 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $a$ or to the vector $c,$ as shown below. So, $a$ or $c$ define the direction of the first principal component. \n\n![](images/4d/4d025f8b6447b7a17e4c5fcfb192dcaf4c2bb1ea3a926961d25e94832972bee1.png)\n\n  \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $b,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8d223a9a8a29f297a88f7d7c98d99776540e0ba0e0f3935892a356e92a8aac9.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514454 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514454 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/dc/dc03c3e8b7769622b363924604f242d0cf7f80eab6e0019eba5a3e95d6bc3c10.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3de56e3-5772-5207-831f-4a8a7dca824e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514454 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $a,$ as shown below. So, $a$ defines the direction of the first principal component. \n\n![](images/4f/4f751057ec2ba51c7132b75ed4e0309269288601ebf19b4f13de0a5b54e6c615.png)\n\n  \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $b,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c272bdfda7665b4892155335ead5cf79b08b98e19a04a6211f2a628a0161c28.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514467 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514467 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514467 #edb/ref 1071 "A meteorologist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ at various locations throughout California. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular location. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.667 \\\\ 0.745\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.745 \\\\ 0.667\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the meteorologist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd2cd7be-06ce-5ffb-8860-cd5d1eb5c130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514467 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/f7/f7c823606bbbe77326819df5d487b5e0019dd7439b46124547f71681b9484f6d.png)\n\n \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.667 & -0.745 \\\\ 0.745 & 0.667\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.667 & -0.745 \\\\ 0.745 & 0.667\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2} & -0.745{x}_{1}+0.667{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
